  "fixture_kind": "public_exact-list_card_data",
  "rulings": [
    {
      "comment": "Because Wastes is a basic land, you can include as many of them as you like in your Constructed decks.",
      "oracle_id": "05d24b0c-904a-46b6-b42a-96a4d91a0dd4",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "In Limited events (including Sealed Deck and Booster Draft), Wastes must be in your card pool to be included in your deck. You can't add Wastes to your card pool in the same way that you can add other basic lands.",
      "oracle_id": "05d24b0c-904a-46b6-b42a-96a4d91a0dd4",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Wastes is not a land type. If something asks you to name a land type, you can't choose Wastes.",
      "oracle_id": "05d24b0c-904a-46b6-b42a-96a4d91a0dd4",
      "published_at": "2021-03-19",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Unlike most dual lands, this land has two basic land types. It's not basic, so cards such as District Guide can't find it, but it does have the appropriate land types for effects such as that of Drowned Catacomb (from the Ixalan set).",
      "oracle_id": "17039058-822d-409f-938c-b727a366ba63",
      "published_at": "2018-10-05",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If an effect puts this land onto the battlefield tapped, you may pay 2 life, but it still enters tapped.",
      "oracle_id": "17039058-822d-409f-938c-b727a366ba63",
      "published_at": "2025-11-17",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "This land has two basic land types. It's not basic, so cards such as Tend the Sprigs can't find it, but it does have the appropriate land types for effects such as that of Kishla Village (from the Tarkir: Dragonstorm release).",
      "oracle_id": "17039058-822d-409f-938c-b727a366ba63",
      "published_at": "2025-11-17",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Dictate of the Twin Gods applies to any damage, not just combat damage. It also doesn't matter who controls the source of the damage that's being dealt.",
      "oracle_id": "3d960d33-623a-4415-ae00-f8cffbc15f5a",
      "published_at": "2014-04-26",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If more than one Dictate of the Twin Gods is on the battlefield, damage dealt will double for each one (two of them will end up multiplying the damage by four, three of them by eight, and four of them by sixteen).",
      "oracle_id": "3d960d33-623a-4415-ae00-f8cffbc15f5a",
      "published_at": "2014-04-26",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If multiple effects modify how damage will be dealt, the player being dealt damage or the controller of the permanent being dealt damage chooses the order to apply the effects. For example, the ability of Decorated Griffin says “Prevent the next 1 combat damage that would be dealt to you this turn.” Suppose you would be dealt 3 combat damage and you activate the ability of Decorated Griffin. You can either (a) prevent 1 damage first and then let Dictate of the Twin Gods's effect double the remaining 2 damage, for a result of being dealt 4 damage, or (b) double the damage to 6 and then prevent 1 damage, for a result of being dealt 5 damage.",
      "oracle_id": "3d960d33-623a-4415-ae00-f8cffbc15f5a",
      "published_at": "2014-04-26",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The source of the damage doesn't change. A spell that deals damage will specify the source of the damage, often the spell itself. An ability that deals damage will also specify the source of the damage, although the ability itself will never be that source. Often the source of the ability is also the source of the damage.",
      "oracle_id": "3d960d33-623a-4415-ae00-f8cffbc15f5a",
      "published_at": "2014-04-26",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "This counts the lands on resolution.",
      "oracle_id": "4ccc1ac5-db90-4fc9-9795-7afaf7466c3e",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The controller of Vedalken Orrery can cast nonland artifact, creature, enchantment, and sorcery cards any time they could cast an instant. This includes on other players' turns and during the upkeep step, draw step, combat phase, and end step.",
      "oracle_id": "60d640a4-032b-4279-ac75-8f200ca776fb",
      "published_at": "2004-12-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "This applies only to casting spells. It does not, for example, change when you may activate abilities that can only be activated \"any time you could cast a sorcery\".",
      "oracle_id": "60d640a4-032b-4279-ac75-8f200ca776fb",
      "published_at": "2010-06-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If a spell or ability damages multiple things, divide up the damage before applying this effect. This means you can’t normally end up with an odd amount of damage on something.",
      "oracle_id": "68715465-6cf9-4006-87e9-31f227fe9ed3",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If you have two of these on the battlefield, the damage is multiplied by 4.",
      "oracle_id": "68715465-6cf9-4006-87e9-31f227fe9ed3",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The multiplied damage counts in all ways as if it came from the original source. Furnace of Rath is not the source.",
      "oracle_id": "68715465-6cf9-4006-87e9-31f227fe9ed3",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The trample rules cause damage to be divided before it is doubled.",
      "oracle_id": "68715465-6cf9-4006-87e9-31f227fe9ed3",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If multiple effects modify how damage will be dealt, the player who would be dealt damage or the controller of the creature that would be dealt damage chooses the order to apply the effects. For example, Mending Hands says, “Prevent the next 4 damage that would be dealt to any target this turn.” Suppose a spell would deal 5 damage to a player who has cast Mending Hands targeting themselves. That player can either (a) prevent 4 damage first and then let Furnace of Rath double the remaining 1 damage, taking 2 damage, or (b) double the damage to 10 and then prevent 4 damage, taking 6 damage.",
      "oracle_id": "68715465-6cf9-4006-87e9-31f227fe9ed3",
      "published_at": "2005-08-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Although Darksteel Citadel is an artifact, it can't be cast as a spell. Playing it follows the normal rules for playing a land.",
      "oracle_id": "8dc067bf-f78f-4ac4-b6e7-b305c42cf0bc",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If the copy says that it affects \"you,\" it affects the controller of the copy, not the controller of the original spell. Similarly, if the copy says that it affects an \"opponent,\" it affects an opponent of the copy's controller, not an opponent of the original spell's controller.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f55890-31c5-4ed4-a2cd-7a4a9f05f8ca",
      "published_at": "2010-08-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If the spell Reverberate copies has an X whose value was determined as it was cast (like Earthquake does), the copy has the same value of X.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f55890-31c5-4ed4-a2cd-7a4a9f05f8ca",
      "published_at": "2010-08-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If the spell Reverberate copies is modal (that is, it says \"Choose one —\" or the like), the copy will have the same mode. You can't choose a different one.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f55890-31c5-4ed4-a2cd-7a4a9f05f8ca",
      "published_at": "2010-08-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Reverberate can target (and copy) any instant or sorcery spell, not just one with targets. It doesn't matter who controls it.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f55890-31c5-4ed4-a2cd-7a4a9f05f8ca",
      "published_at": "2010-08-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The copy will have the same targets as the spell it's copying unless you choose new ones. You may change any number of the targets, including all of them or none of them. If, for one of the targets, you can't choose a new legal target, then it remains unchanged (even if the current target is illegal).",
      "oracle_id": "a1f55890-31c5-4ed4-a2cd-7a4a9f05f8ca",
      "published_at": "2010-08-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "When Reverberate resolves, it creates a copy of a spell. You control the copy. That copy is created on the stack, so it's not \"cast.\" Abilities that trigger when a player casts a spell won't trigger. The copy will then resolve like a normal spell, after players get a chance to cast spells and activate abilities.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f55890-31c5-4ed4-a2cd-7a4a9f05f8ca",
      "published_at": "2010-08-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "You can't choose to pay any additional costs for the copy. However, effects based on any additional costs that were paid for the original spell are copied as though those same costs were paid for the copy too. For example, if a player sacrifices a 3/3 creature to cast Fling, and you copy it with Reverberate, the copy of Fling will also deal 3 damage to its target.",
      "oracle_id": "a1f55890-31c5-4ed4-a2cd-7a4a9f05f8ca",
      "published_at": "2010-08-15",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "The enchanted land loses its existing land types and any abilities printed on it. It now has the land type Island and has the ability to tap to produce {U}. Spreading Seas doesn't change the enchanted land's name or whether it's legendary, basic, or snow.",
      "oracle_id": "af32757c-9d3c-4f6c-a6fa-d5729a440382",
      "published_at": "2009-10-01",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Abilities that Slivers grant, as well as power/toughness boosts, are cumulative. However, for some abilities, like indestructible and the ability granted by Belligerent Sliver, having more than one instance of the ability doesn’t provide any additional benefit.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Damage dealt to a Sliver with indestructible is still marked on that creature. If it has lethal damage marked on it and it loses indestructible (perhaps because Sliver Hivelord leaves the battlefield), it will be destroyed.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "If you change the creature type of a Sliver you control so it’s no longer a Sliver, it will no longer be affected by its own ability. Its ability will continue to affect other Sliver creatures you control.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Slivers in this set affect only Sliver creatures you control. They don’t grant bonuses to your opponents’ Slivers. This is the same way Slivers from the Magic 2014 core set worked, while Slivers in earlier sets granted bonuses to all Slivers.",
      "oracle_id": "e4fe33e7-b952-4152-8c83-81d948756d2f",
      "published_at": "2014-07-18",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Number of Islands is counted on resolution and not on announcement.",
      "oracle_id": "ef295a34-0325-49dd-87a6-546dde395082",
      "published_at": "2004-10-04",
      "source": "wotc"
    },
    {
      "comment": "Unlike most dual lands, this land has two basic land types. It's not basic, so cards such as District Guide can't find it, but it does have the appropriate land types for effects such as that of Drowned Catacomb (from the Ixalan set).",
      "oracle_id": "f413a83d-a40d-434c-b20a-4c707c0527fa",
      "published_at": "2018-10-05",
      "source": "wotc"
